    "lawCode": "Amd §4903, Pub Health L; amd §4903, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4792\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law and the insurance law, in relation\nto utilization review of coverage of nursing home care following an\ninpatient hospital admission\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nRequires a utilization review of a request for nursing home care after\nan inpatient hospital admission to be completed within 24 hours of the\nrequest.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends paragraphs (a) and (c) of subdivision 3 of section 4903\nof the public health law as it relates to utilization review.  This\nsection adds nursing home care to the list of services where the request\nfor a utilization review must be completed within 24 hours of the\nrequest.\n\nSection 2 amends paragraphs 1 and 3 of subsection (c) of section 4903 of\nthe insurance law as it relates to utilization review. This section adds\nnursing home care to the list of services where the request for a utili-\nzation review must be completed within 24 hours of the request.\n\nSection 3 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany patients require home care or skilled nursing home care upon\ndischarge from the hospital. Hospitals increasingly experience delays in\nobtaining authorizations from insurers for post-acute care. Since most\ninsurers pay hospitals based on diagnosis-related group systems, it does\nnot cost the insurer more money to have patients stay in the hospital\nfor a few extra days and doing so can reduce insurer costs for post-a-\ncute care. Delays in authorizing nursing home care are not good for\npatients, nor for hospitals trying to operate as efficiently as possi-\nble.\n\nState law has been modified twice in recent years to tighten timeframes\nfor authorization for post-acute home care and substance abuse services.\nThis legislation will extend the tightened timeframe to skilled nursing\nhome services, requiring insurers to make determinations on requests for\nnursing home care following an inpatient admission within 24 hours of\nreceipt of a request as long as the request is made at least 24 hours\nprior to discharge from the hospital. This change is particularly impor-\ntant now that the Medicaid nursing home benefit has moved from Medicaid\nfee-for-service to managed care.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: S1810/A3059 Gottfried - Vetoed 12/20/19\n\n2019-2020: S7525/A9086 Gottfried\n\n2021-2022: S2125/No Same As\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall be effective ninety days after becoming law.\n",
